          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "**What the retrieved sources support**\n\n- **Delayed or no antibiotics for mild cystitis is where authorities differ.** Some European guidance offers symptom treatment with delayed or no antibiotics as a shared decision, because some episodes resolve without them. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, citing symptom duration and the risk of kidney involvement. The source says the disagreement is about how much antibiotic exposure is worth avoiding, not about the facts. (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance, *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection*.)\n- **Cranberry products and extra fluids** are described as having limited and mixed evidence. The same source does not say whether guidance recommends them. (Same document.)\n- **For frequent recurrence**, the guidance lists behavior changes, vaginal estrogen after menopause, and antibiotic prophylaxis where recurrence is frequent and disabling. Prophylaxis is weighed against resistance. Recurrence is conventionally defined as two or more infections in six months, or three or more in a year. (Same document.)\n- **Diagnosis:** with typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, empiric treatment without culture is described as appropriate. A urine dipstick adds little in that group. (Same document.)\n- **Scope:** pregnancy, men, catheters, structural problems, stones, weakened immunity, and suspected kidney involvement are managed as complicated infection on a different pathway. (Same document.)\n\n**What the retrieved sources do not answer**\n\nThe search returned nothing specific on ibuprofen or other symptom relief, methenamine, D-mannose, or the strength of evidence for watchful waiting in UTI. I have not filled those gaps from memory. The delayed-prescription passage that came back concerns upper respiratory infections, not UTI, so I have not applied it here.\n\n**Points to take to a clinician**\n\n- Whether a delayed-prescription approach suits your situation, and what symptoms would change the plan.\n- Whether symptom relief such as ibuprofen is appropriate for you. Ask about doses for your own case.\n- If you have had repeated infections, what prevention options fit your history.\n\nThis summary comes from educational guidance excerpts rather than full guideline texts, so check the primary guideline before relying on any detail.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
